From 3423c0e34906ab12c2a45bafee6d26d7ac18fa2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leandro Forain <leandro.forain@instructure.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 01:12:34 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] jobs: continue and signal whole process groups, keep the
 terminal, report background pids

Three job-control defects, all visible on Linux with the interactive tests
added here and on any login console:

* `fg`/`bg` of a stopped pipeline sent SIGCONT to a single pid (the group
  leader). A pipeline is stopped as a group, so every other member stayed
  stopped and `fg` then waited on them for ever, leaving the shell hung
  with the terminal handed to a job that could never finish. Job signals
  now go to the job's process group (`killpg`, as bash does) when it has
  one, and to each known process otherwise (job control off). The job's
  group is taken from its spawned children rather than assumed to equal
  the first pid, and `kill %n` uses the same path.

* A pipeline that failed after a member had already taken the terminal --
  a later member failing to spawn, an expansion error, a failed wait --
  propagated the error without taking the terminal back. The terminal's
  foreground group was then a dead group, the shell's next read was a
  background read (SIGTTIN, or EIO for an orphaned login shell) and the
  shell was wedged. `Pipeline::execute` and `fg` now restore the shell's
  group on every path, like bash's give_terminal_to(shell_pgrp).

* `cmd &` printed `[1] <pid unknown>`: the announcement was formatted
  before the background task had spawned anything. The async pid slot is
  now a resolvable cell; the parent waits for the task to decide what its
  first command is (first external process spawned, a loop entered, or
  the task finished) before announcing, so `[n] pid` and `$!` carry the
  real pid immediately. The slot also carries the process group so
  `fg`/`bg`/`kill %n` of such a job address the whole group.
